<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josip Broz Tito</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josip Broz Tito led the Communist Partisans against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, setting up his first headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a> in 1941. He ruled socialist Yugoslavia from 1945 until his death, broke with Stalin in 1948, developed workers' self-management and helped found the Non-Aligned Movement. The federation he held together broke up a decade after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito.jpg" alt="Josip Broz Tito" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josip_Broz_Tito_uniform_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It brought together Slovenes, Croats, Serbs, Bosniaks, Montenegrins and Macedonians under one government for the first time, and many of its borders became the internal and external borders of <b>Tito</b>’s federation and of today’s successor states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Two resistances arose: the royalist Yugoslav Army in the Homeland and the Communist Partisans under <b>Josip Broz Tito</b>, a pan-Yugoslav movement preaching the 'brotherhood and unity' of the Yugoslav nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1956 <b>Tito</b>, Nehru and Nasser signed the Declaration of Brijuni in Yugoslavia, and in September 1961, on Tito’s initiative, the first Conference of Heads of State or Government of Non-Aligned Countries met in Belgrade.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Governments modelled on the Soviet Union took power with Soviet help in Bulgaria, Czechoslovakia, East Germany, Poland, Hungary and Romania after the Second World War; <b>Tito</b>’s Yugoslavia broke with Stalin in 1948, and the Cuban revolutionaries who overthrew Batista in 1959 became Marxist–Leninist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Partisans' With Marshal Tito of 1943 originally named <b>Tito</b> together with Stalin, and after the split of 1948 that version was no longer performed or printed.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Axis invasion of Yugoslavia in April 1941, the anti-royalist Partisans of <b>Josip Broz Tito</b> and the Communist Party avoided the royal anthem and sang &quot;Hey, Slavs&quot; instead, at both sessions of AVNOJ, their legislative body.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kumrovec, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.08,15.68&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Kumrovec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4920 541 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Josip Broz Tito"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5370 811h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5370 811h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.08,15.68&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
